    "translation": "Under the work-style reform that set out to correct long working hours, caps were placed on overtime, and statistically measured working hours have been steadily decreasing. Considering that the situation was once so grave that the word 'karoshi' (death from overwork) became known internationally, this looks like a change to be welcomed. Companies that switch off the office lights at closing time, or strictly record and manage departure times, are no longer rare.\nHowever, when we look at conditions on the ground, the story is not so simple. With the total volume of work unchanged and only clocked hours cut, many report that unrecorded take-home work has increased and that the intensity of labor during working hours has actually risen. It is also pointed out that the burden is being shunted onto managers, who tend to fall outside the regulations. 'Being told to go home early doesn't make the work disappear' — the lament from the front lines is a grave one. Just because the statistics have improved, it does not follow that workers' burdens have grown lighter.\nThat said, I do not mean that the regulations themselves were meaningless. Without a legally enforceable cap, the corporate culture that took long hours for granted would never have changed. The problem lies in the illusion that regulating hours completed the reform. Institutions can be an entry point for changing attitudes, but they will not, by themselves, change the substance of how we work.\nThe real objective is a review of the workload itself and a shift to systems that evaluate results rather than hours. Tasks that persist as mere custom — meetings, document preparation — must also be reviewed without sacred cows. If we merely preach shorter hours without the resolve to decide what to stop doing, the burden simply moves somewhere invisible. Regulation is not the destination of reform; it is merely the starting point.",
