    "translation": "As the sense of crisis over climate change grows, individual efforts — bringing your own bag, diligently saving electricity — are energetically encouraged. The claim that each person's mindfulness matters seems reasonable at first glance, leaving apparently no room for objection. At school and at work alike, environmental slogans are posted and the purchase of energy-saving products is encouraged.\nHowever, the greater part of greenhouse gas emissions derives from the workings of society itself: energy policy and industrial structure. Indeed, some studies find that a considerable share of the world's emissions derives from the activities of a very small group of companies. Thinking that relies solely on individual goodwill risks shifting onto consumers a responsibility that properly belongs to those structures, and may even delay fundamental reform. If large-scale emissions escape scrutiny in the shadow of appeals to save energy, that can only be called putting the cart before the horse. The criticism of companies that advertise environmental concern without substance is not unrelated to this pattern.\nThis is not to say that individual action is meaningless. Consumers' choices move companies through the market, and awareness cultivated in daily practice eventually grows into concern for policy. Looking back at history, great social transformations have often begun with changes in the awareness of nameless individuals. The individual and the structure are not opposed; they stand in a circular relationship, each moving the other.\nWhat is essential is to value individual effort while not letting it end there. Only when we go beyond shopping choices and press for institutional change through voting and speaking out does individual action finally become a force that moves the structure. Only when steady steps and a voice raised toward institutions come together does the road to decarbonization open.",
