    "translation": "Population decline in the regions shows no sign of stopping. Many municipalities compete with one another in subsidies for newcomers and child-rearing support, making the recovery of population their top policy priority. The belief that population is the wellspring of regional vitality still appears unshaken. At migration fairs, municipalities from across the country line up booths, with enthusiasm extending to free housing and even celebration money.\nHowever, as long as the population of the country as a whole keeps shrinking, municipalities snatching residents from one another will not improve the overall situation in the slightest. One town's success in attracting migrants usually comes at the cost of another town's shrinkage. Competition over a limited population is, viewed nationally, nothing but a war of attrition with no winners. The race to pile up subsidies squeezes municipal finances and can even lead to the absurdity of cutting the very resident services that matter.\nThis is not to say that we should merely lament shrinkage and resign ourselves to decline. What is being asked is how to design prosperity that does not presuppose population growth. Rather than straining to maintain facilities and services, we can fold them wisely, consolidate them into hubs, and share roles with neighboring municipalities. Withdrawal can be a strategy for sustainability, not a defeat. Indeed, some regions have consolidated schools and hospitals while actually raising the quality of daily life. What matters is a debate over priorities: what to keep, and what to share.\nSpeaking of shrinkage has long been taboo in politics. But when we face population decline squarely and free ourselves from the notion that it amounts to defeat, then and only then, I suspect, will a realistic hope for the future of the regions come into view. When we let go of the single yardstick of population, other measures of a region's appeal and happiness should come into sight.",
