    "translation": "It has long been said that the spread of smartphones has driven people away from print. Book sales keep falling, and laments over young people's flight from reading never cease. The sight of someone opening a paperback on the train has, at some point, become a rare one.\nHowever, a closer look at the reality suggests that the sheer amount of text people read has, if anything, increased. News, messages, posts — we read something all day long. The problem is not quantity but quality: a mode of reading that consumes fragment after fragment has become dominant, while 'deep reading' — following a long argument and building one's own thoughts in dialogue with the author — is withering. As the experience of spending long stretches with a single book dwindles, so too does the stamina to think persistently about complex problems.\nPut this way, you might expect the conclusion that digital media are the culprit and that we should return to paper books. But that would miss the point. What sustains deep reading is not paper as a material but the habits and environment that keep attention fixed on a single object. Deep reading is possible on a screen; conversely, even with a paper book in front of you, if notifications keep pulling at your attention, only fragmentary reading is possible. The question, in short, is how to rearrange an environment surrounded by designs that compete for our attention.\nDeep reading is not something acquired naturally if left alone. Like swimming or a musical instrument, it is a cultural skill gained through deliberate training and protected by one's environment. Rather than debating old media versus new, what we ought to be thinking about is how to cultivate this skill and hand it on to the next generation. Deliberately setting aside unbroken time for reading, at school and at home, would be the first step.",
